**user**:
> I am developing a stock market prediction ecosystem, and my main problem right now is that I need to limit the number of tickers I track. Downloading the tickers each day (I just get opening, closing, and current prices) takes time and pushes me against some query limits in Julia's YFinance package. So my main question is, how can I identify high-quality stock to track? I want stocks that see good trading, but I don't want to be limited to, e.g., the S&P 500.
**assistant**:
> Do not define your tracking universe as “the most famous stocks.” Define it as a **liquid, investable, diverse, and stable security universe** that you can maintain cheaply.
> 
> The key distinction is:
> 
> - **Tradability / data quality:** Can you reliably obtain and act on a price signal?
> - **Business quality:** Is the company financially durable/profitable?
> - **Predictive value:** Does including this ticker improve your strategy after costs and realistic execution?
> 
> A stock can be excellent on one dimension and poor on the others.
> 
> ## Recommended approach: a tiered, rules-based universe
> 
> Rather than tracking every listed stock or only an index, maintain:
> 
> 1. **Core universe** — highly liquid stocks downloaded daily.
> 2. **Discovery universe** — a rotating set of less-liquid but potentially useful stocks, refreshed less often.
> 3. **Candidate master list** — broad listing universe, screened monthly or quarterly.
> 
> For example:
> 
> | Tier | Purpose | Typical size | Refresh frequency |
> |---|---:|---:|---:|
> | Core | Production predictions / realistic trading | 500–1,500 | Daily prices |
> | Discovery | Find emerging opportunities and test coverage | 200–1,000 | Weekly or monthly prices |
> | Candidate list | Broad screening universe | 3,000–8,000 US common stocks | Monthly/quarterly metadata and liquidity screen |
> 
> This lets you cover much more than the S&P 500 without paying to query everything every day.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Start from a broad, non-index-constrained universe
> 
> For a US-focused system, begin with all eligible **NYSE, Nasdaq, and NYSE American common stocks**, then filter. Include or exclude ADRs deliberately.
> 
> Usually exclude:
> 
> - ETFs, mutual funds, closed-end funds, ETNs
> - preferred shares
> - warrants, rights, units
> - leveraged/inverse products
> - OTC stocks, unless you have a specific OTC strategy
> - extremely recent IPOs, perhaps those with under 3–6 months of trading history
> - stocks consistently priced below, say, \$3–5
> 
> You can generate the starting list from exchange symbol directories, a commercial reference-data source, or a free listing source. The point is that the **index is not the source of truth**; it is only one possible source of candidates.
> 
> A useful compromise is to seed the candidate list from the union of:
> 
> - broad US listings;
> - constituents of broad-market ETFs/indexes (Russell 3000, CRSP, S&P 1500, etc., where licensing/data permits);
> - holdings of liquid sector ETFs;
> - optionally, major non-US ADRs.
> 
> Then apply your own eligibility rules.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## The most important filter: dollar liquidity
> 
> Raw share volume is misleading: 10 million shares of a \$1 stock is not equivalent to 10 million shares of a \$100 stock.
> 
> Use **median daily dollar volume**:
> 
> \[
> \mathrm{ADDV}_{i} =
> \operatorname{median}_{t \in \mathrm{last\ 60\ trading\ days}}
> \left(P_{i,t} \times V_{i,t}\right)
> \]
> 
> where \(P\) can be close or typical price.
> 
> I would generally use a 60-day median rather than a mean, since it is less distorted by one news-driven spike.
> 
> Illustrative thresholds:
> 
> | Universe type | 60-day median daily dollar volume |
> |---|---:|
> | Broad but reasonably tradable | > \$2–5 million/day |
> | Strong production universe | > \$10–20 million/day |
> | Institutional-scale or short-horizon strategies | > \$50–100 million/day |
> 
> The right threshold depends on your intended position sizes. A practical rule is that your intended daily trade should be a very small fraction of normal daily dollar volume—often well under 1%, and more conservatively under 0.1–0.25%.
> 
> If you only have daily OHLCV data, dollar volume is probably your best first-order liquidity measure. If you later obtain bid/ask data, add median quoted spread and market depth.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## A sensible eligibility screen
> 
> For each ticker, evaluate eligibility monthly or quarterly:
> 
> ```text
> Eligible if all hold:
> 
> - Common equity on a supported primary exchange
> - At least 180 trading days of price history
> - Price >= $5
> - Market capitalization >= $500 million or $1 billion
> - Median 60-day dollar volume >= $10 million
> - Traded on at least 95% of the past 60 sessions
> - No obvious data-quality flags or prolonged halts
> ```
> 
> The market-cap floor is not strictly necessary, but it removes many securities where prices are fragile, corporate events are frequent, and daily Yahoo-quality data are more likely to be awkward.
> 
> For a more inclusive discovery tier, relax this—for example, \$2 million median daily dollar volume and \$250 million market cap—while keeping it out of the production trading universe until it qualifies.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Rank eligible stocks instead of applying only hard cutoffs
> 
> After eligibility filtering, rank stocks using a composite score. For example:
> 
> \[
> S_i =
> 0.45\,R(\log \mathrm{ADDV}_i)
> + 0.20\,R(\log \mathrm{MarketCap}_i)
> + 0.15\,R(\mathrm{TradingFrequency}_i)
> + 0.10\,R(-\mathrm{Volatility}_i)
> + 0.10\,R(\mathrm{DataCompleteness}_i)
> \]
> 
> where \(R(\cdot)\) is a percentile rank among eligible stocks.
> 
> This produces a stable ranking while retaining flexibility. You could then select:
> 
> - the top 800–1,200 overall;
> - subject to sector caps or minimum sector representation;
> - plus a rotating discovery set.
> 
> I would use **sector quotas or caps**, because a pure liquidity ranking will heavily concentrate in technology, financials, mega-cap consumer, and a few heavily traded speculative names.
> 
> For example:
> 
> ```text
> Select the top 1,000 names by composite score,
> but no sector may exceed 25% of the universe,
> and each sufficiently liquid sector receives at least 30 names.
> ```
> 
> That is not necessarily the ideal trading portfolio; it is a better **research and prediction universe**. It ensures that your model sees different market regimes and business types.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Add business-quality metrics only if they serve your strategy
> 
> If by “high-quality stock” you mean financially strong companies rather than merely liquid securities, add a separate **fundamental quality score**. Typical components include:
> 
> - profitability: ROIC, ROA, gross profitability, operating margin;
> - cash generation: free-cash-flow margin or FCF yield;
> - balance-sheet resilience: net debt/EBITDA, interest coverage;
> - earnings stability: frequency and magnitude of negative earnings surprises;
> - dilution: change in shares outstanding;
> - accounting quality: accruals, cash conversion, abnormal working-capital behavior.
> 
> Keep this separate from liquidity:
> 
> \[
> \mathrm{UniverseScore}_i =
> w_L \mathrm{LiquidityScore}_i
> + w_Q \mathrm{FundamentalQualityScore}_i
> + w_D \mathrm{DataScore}_i
> \]
> 
> However, I would not filter too aggressively on fundamental quality at the universe-construction stage unless your intended strategy specifically targets quality. Otherwise, you risk excluding the distressed, cyclical, or turnaround stocks where some predictive signals may matter most.
> 
> A useful compromise:
> 
> - **Core tradable universe:** based mainly on liquidity, price, age, and data completeness.
> - **Quality label/features:** used by the prediction or portfolio model, not necessarily as a hard inclusion rule.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Avoid daily reconstitution
> 
> Do not let a stock enter or leave the universe every day merely because it crossed a liquidity threshold.
> 
> Use:
> 
> - **monthly eligibility review**;
> - **quarterly full reconstitution**;
> - an entry threshold stricter than the retention threshold.
> 
> For example:
> 
> ```text
> New entrant: median 60-day dollar volume >= $15m
> Existing member remains: median 60-day dollar volume >= $8m
> ```
> 
> This hysteresis prevents churn and makes both your data pipeline and backtests more stable.
> 
> You can still remove a ticker immediately for delisting, acquisition, a long trading halt, or persistent missing data.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Design the data pipeline so the universe, not Yahoo, is the bottleneck
> 
> Your current constraint sounds partly architectural: you should not need to re-download a ticker’s entire history every day.
> 
> Maintain a local, analysis-ready price store—ideally partitioned Parquet or DuckDB—containing at least:
> 
> ```text
> ticker
> date
> open
> high
> low
> close
> adjusted_close
> volume
> source_timestamp
> ```
> 
> Then:
> 
> 1. Download full or long history only when a ticker first enters your tracked universe.
> 2. On normal days, request only the missing dates since the last stored observation.
> 3. Query intraday/current prices only for the relatively small set of names actually eligible for trading that day.
> 4. Persist raw responses or normalized daily bars locally.
> 5. Refresh the universe screen monthly, rather than repeatedly querying metadata daily.
> 
> For most daily or multi-day prediction systems, the prior official close plus today’s open is vastly more important and reproducible than a constantly refreshed “current price.” Treat the current price as an **execution-state input**, not as a core historical feature that must be collected for every candidate ticker.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## A practical initial specification
> 
> If I were building your first production version, I would start here:
> 
> ```text
> Market:
>   US-listed common equities, including eligible ADRs
> 
> Core universe:
>   1,000 stocks
> 
> Entry criteria:
>   - price >= $5
>   - market cap >= $1bn
>   - at least 180 trading days since IPO/listing
>   - median 60-day dollar volume >= $15m
>   - traded on >=
