> For designing the tracking/universe system, I would want documentation or example responses for a fairly small subset first. The central question is: **which service should be authoritative for which kind of data?**
> 
> My preliminary recommendation would be:
> 
> - **Schwab:** authoritative for current quotes, historical bars, optionability/options, market hours, and perhaps tradability-oriented metadata.
> - **YFinance.jl:** useful as a broad discovery and supplementary fundamental/news source, but not the sole production source for price data if Schwab is available.
> - **Your local database:** authoritative for all historical data once collected.
> 
> ## Highest-priority documentation
> 
> ### 1. Schwab `GET /quotes` — essential
> 
> This is likely the most important endpoint for a daily system, because batch quote retrieval may solve much of the ticker-query problem.
> 
> Please get documentation for:
> 
> - Maximum number of symbols per request
> - How multiple symbols are passed (`symbols=AAPL,MSFT`, repeated query parameters, etc.)
> - Supported `fields` or projection parameters
> - Response fields and nested structure, especially:
>   - symbol and asset type
>   - bid / ask / bid size / ask size
>   - last price
>   - mark price
>   - open, high, low, close
>   - prior close
>   - total volume
>   - quote timestamp and trade timestamp
>   - exchange / exchange name
>   - 52-week high/low, if available
>   - volatility or other risk fields, if available
>   - market capitalization, shares outstanding, and P/E, if provided
>   - whether a symbol is delayed or real-time
> - Whether the endpoint returns partial successes if some symbols are invalid/unavailable
> - Rate limits: per request, per minute, per day, and concurrent requests
> - Entitlement requirements for real-time quotes
> - Whether pricing fields are available outside regular hours, and how pre-/post-market prices are labeled.
> 
> A representative JSON response for a few equities—e.g. `AAPL, MSFT, BRK.B, SPY`—would be especially useful. It will reveal symbol normalization issues early.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### 2. Schwab `GET /pricehistory` — essential
> 
> This should be the primary endpoint for backfilling and incrementally updating daily prices, assuming its limits and retention are workable.
> 
> Please get:
> 
> - All request parameters:
>   - `symbol`
>   - `periodType`, `period`
>   - `frequencyType`, `frequency`
>   - `startDate`, `endDate`
>   - `needExtendedHoursData`
>   - `needPreviousClose`
>   - any others
> - Valid combinations of period and frequency.
> - Historical retention limits, particularly for:
>   - daily bars;
>   - weekly/monthly bars;
>   - 1-minute, 5-minute, 15-minute, etc. intraday bars.
> - Whether dates/timestamps are UTC, US/Eastern, exchange-local, or epoch milliseconds.
> - The meaning of bar fields:
>   - `open`, `high`, `low`, `close`, `volume`, `datetime`;
>   - adjusted versus unadjusted prices;
>   - split adjustment;
>   - dividend adjustment.
> - Whether the returned `close` is official close, last regular-session trade, or something else.
> - Whether a result includes current/incomplete daily candles while the market is open.
> - Maximum request frequency / rate limits.
> - Behavior for delisted tickers, renamed securities, mergers, and symbols with no price history.
> - Example daily-bar response and example intraday-bar response.
> 
> This endpoint determines whether we can calculate rolling liquidity measures locally:
> 
> \[
> \mathrm{median\ dollar\ volume}_{60d}
> =
> \operatorname{median}\left(
> \mathrm{close}_t \times \mathrm{volume}_t
> \right).
> \]
> 
> If so, we may not need a separate external liquidity-data provider.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### 3. Schwab `GET /instruments` — essential for universe construction
> 
> This is the endpoint most likely to help identify the broad candidate universe and classify securities correctly.
> 
> Please obtain documentation for:
> 
> - Valid `projection` values and their intended use:
>   - symbol search;
>   - descriptive search;
>   - fundamental data;
>   - regex search;
>   - CUSIP search;
>   - any “all” or listing-related projection.
> - Whether it supports a way to retrieve **all US-listed equities**, or only search-based discovery.
> - Maximum result count and pagination behavior.
> - Whether the response includes:
>   - asset type;
>   - security type/subtype;
>   - exchange;
>   - CUSIP;
>   - ISIN, if available;
>   - description;
>   - whether the security is an ETF, mutual fund, ADR, preferred stock, warrant, right, or ordinary common stock;
>   - IPO/listing date;
>   - delisting status;
>   - fundamental fields such as market capitalization, shares outstanding, P/E, dividend yield, etc.
> - Exact semantics and update frequency of any fundamental data.
> - An example response for:
>   - an ordinary US common stock (`AAPL`);
>   - an ETF (`SPY`);
>   - an ADR (`TSM`);
>   - a special ticker class if available, such as a preferred share or warrant.
> 
> The major design issue: if Schwab cannot provide a bulk US equity symbol directory, then it is probably not the right source for the initial candidate universe—even if it is the right source for prices and quotes.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### 4. YFinance.jl `get_all_symbols` and `get_symbols` — high priority
> 
> For these two, I want to know whether they can practically support broad discovery.
> 
> #### `get_all_symbols(market)`
> 
> Please find:
> 
> - Valid values of `market`.
> - What each result actually contains:
>   - ticker only?
>   - company name?
>   - exchange?
>   - quote type/security class?
>   - active versus inactive status?
> - Whether it covers all US exchanges or only a Yahoo-defined market universe.
> - Whether ETFs, funds, warrants, crypto, indices, and OTC symbols are mixed in.
> - Whether pagination/limits exist.
> - Whether the result is cached by the package or always downloaded.
> - Any documented or observed query/rate limits.
> - Whether the symbols are Yahoo symbols specifically—and therefore whether they require normalization before use at Schwab.
> 
> #### `get_symbols(search_term)`
> 
> Please obtain:
> 
> - Search-result fields: symbol, exchange, quote type, name, market state, etc.
> - Maximum number of returned matches.
> - Whether it is suitable only for human-style text search, or could be used systematically.
> - How ambiguous ticker formats are represented, especially:
>   - `BRK.B` versus `BRK-B`;
>   - preferred shares;
>   - foreign listings and ADRs;
>   - class shares.
> 
> My expectation is that `get_symbols` is not useful for bulk universe construction; it is primarily a convenience for resolving unknown symbols. But documentation/examples will confirm that.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Important, but secondary, documentation
> 
> ### 5. Schwab `GET /chains` and `/expirationchain`
> 
> These matter if options data will inform:
> 
> - optionability;
> - implied volatility;
> - put/call skew;
> - open interest;
> - unusual options activity;
> - forward-looking market expectations.
> 
> For now, get documentation on:
> 
> - all request parameters;
> - response schema;
> - whether delayed versus real-time Greeks/quotes are distinguished;
> - strike/expiration limits;
> - rate limits;
> - whether open interest and volume are provided;
> - contract symbol formatting;
> - whether `expirationchain` is redundant with expiration information returned by `chains`.
> 
> I would **not** query chains daily for every tracked stock initially. That becomes expensive quickly. A better later design is to retrieve options data only for:
> 
> - stocks already in the core universe;
> - optionable stocks above a liquidity threshold;
> - perhaps a subset selected by your equity model or event calendar.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### 6. Schwab `GET /movers`
> 
> Potentially useful as a compact discovery signal, but not as the basis of your universe.
> 
> Please get:
> 
> - Supported `symbol_id` values—e.g., `$SPX`, `$DJI`, `$COMPX`, sector indices, etc.
> - Time horizons supported.
> - Whether “movers” are ranked by percentage change, volume, dollar volume, unusual activity, or another criterion.
> - Maximum results.
> - Exchange/asset-class coverage.
> - Whether it runs in pre-market/post-market.
> 
> This can populate a small rotating “attention” or discovery tier, but it will systematically emphasize event-driven stocks. That can be valuable for a short-horizon model, but it is not representative of the market.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### 7. Schwab `GET /markets` / `/markets/{market_id}`
> 
> This is operationally important, though not central to security selection.
> 
> We need:
> 
> - Market identifiers for equities, options, etc.
> - Session timestamps and time zone.
> - Holiday and early-close behavior.
> - Whether the response distinguishes pre-market, regular trading, and post-market.
> - How far ahead the calendar is available.
> 
> This should define your scheduler. For example, daily close ingestion should not run at a fixed clock time without checking that the relevant market session has completed.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Lower priority for the initial universe system
> 
> ### YFinance.jl `get_quoteSummary`
> 
> This may be useful for fundamental quality features, but it is potentially expensive and uneven across tickers.
> 
> Please get the documentation for:
> 
> - All available `item` values.
> - Whether multiple items can be requested together.
> - Which items contain:
