> ## One subtle issue: `remaining` can still include current stocks
> 
> In the existing classification loop, non-overdue current stocks are added to `remaining`:
> 
> ```python
> if ticker in current_in_candidate_list:
>     if age >= current_after:
>         current_overdue.append(ticker)
>     else:
>         remaining.append(ticker)
> ```
> 
> So if there are not enough ordinary overdue candidates, the final:
> 
> ```python
> take_from(remaining)
> ```
> 
> can still select additional current tickers and violate the 5% intention.
> 
> To enforce the cap across **all current tickers**, alter that block to *not* put current stocks in `remaining`:
> 
> ```python
> if ticker in current_in_candidate_list:
>     if age >= current_after:
>         current_overdue.append(ticker)
>     # Otherwise do nothing: current tickers not overdue should not be
>     # sampled as ordinary fallback candidates.
> else:
>     if age >= candidate_after:
>         ordinary_overdue.append(ticker)
>     else:
>         remaining.append(ticker)
> ```
> 
> With those edits:
> 
> - at most 5% of your present tracked universe is evaluated per run;
> - those slots go to overdue current tickers;
> - remaining review capacity is filled by new/non-current candidates;
> - current stocks cannot slip back into the batch through the fallback pool.
> 
> ### Small-universe choice
> 
> Using `math.floor()` is strictly faithful to “at most 5%,” but means a universe with fewer than 20 currently tracked securities evaluates zero current tickers per run.
> 
> If you instead want at least one current ticker reviewed whenever any are overdue, use:
> 
> ```python
> max_current_reviews = max(
>     1,
>     math.floor(
>         len(current_in_candidate_list)
>         * float(review["max_current_review_fraction"])
>     ),
> )
> ```
> 
> That is generally more practical, but technically exceeds 5% for small universes.
